$$
(n + (n + 1) + (n + 2)) = 3n + 3
$$

Since $3n + 3 = 3(n + 1)$, we can see that the sum is indeed divisible by 3. Therefore, regardless of the value of $n$, the sum of any three consecutive integers will always be divisible by 3.

This approach uses basic arithmetic and divisibility properties to show that the sum is always a multiple of 3. The key idea is recognizing that the sum of three consecutive integers is three times the sum of one more integer, which is a multiple of 3.

By applying this reasoning, we can confidently conclude that the sum of any three consecutive integers is divisible by 3.
